Hand-written SQL migrations, applied by Alembic.

There is no autogenerate here: the app has no SQLAlchemy models — it is raw psycopg and
pydantic — so there is nothing for Alembic to diff the database against. Write the SQL
yourself.

    just db-revision "add recipes table"     # creates an empty revision to fill in
    just db-migrate                          # apply to the local database
    just db-migrate-sql                      # print the SQL without applying it

Conventions worth keeping:

  * One transaction per revision (env.py sets transaction_per_migration). Anything that
    cannot run inside a transaction — CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY is the usual one — needs
    `with op.get_context().autocommit_block():`.
  * Additive first. Add a nullable column, deploy, backfill, then tighten. A migration
    that a rollback of the app cannot survive is a migration that makes rollback
    impossible.
  * Always write downgrade(). It is rarely run in anger, but writing it is what forces
    you to notice a change that cannot be undone.
